WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.485
When building a complex
application using an LLM, one of the

2
00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:11.730
important but sometimes
tricky steps is how do you

3
00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.254
evaluate how well
your application is doing?

4
00:00:14.254 --> 00:00:16.417
Is it meeting some accuracy criteria?

5
00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:20.110
And also, if you decide
to change your implementation,

6
00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.411
maybe swap in a different LLM,
or change the strategy

7
00:00:23.411 --> 00:00:27.703
of how you use a vector database or
something else to retrieve chunks,

8
00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:30.344
or change some other
parameters of your system,

9
00:00:30.344 --> 00:00:34.306
how do you know if you're
making it better or worse? In

10
00:00:34.306 --> 00:00:36.617
this video, Harrison
will dive into some

11
00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:39.918
frameworks on how to
think about evaluating a LLM-based application,

12
00:00:39.918 --> 00:00:42.229
as well
as some tools to help

13
00:00:42.229 --> 00:00:43.220
you do that.

14
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.397
These applications are really
chains and sequences of

15
00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:48.663
a lot of different steps.
And so honestly, part of the first

16
00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:50.841
thing that you should
do is just understand

17
00:00:50.841 --> 00:00:53.835
what exactly is going
in and coming out of each step.

18
00:00:53.835 --> 00:00:57.101
And so some of the tools can really
just be thought of

19
00:00:57.101 --> 00:00:59.006
as visualizers or debuggers
in that vein.

20
00:00:59.948 --> 00:01:03.462
But it's often really useful to
get a more holistic picture on a lot

21
00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:06.223
of different data points
of how the model is doing. And

22
00:01:06.223 --> 00:01:09.486
one way to do that is
by looking at things by eye. But

23
00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:12.247
there's also this really cool
idea of using language models themselves

24
00:01:12.247 --> 00:01:13.753
and chains themselves
to evaluate other

25
00:01:13.753 --> 00:01:15.510
language models, and other
chains, and other

26
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.020
applications. And we'll dive a
bunch into that as well.

27
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.610
So, lots of cool topics,
and I find that with a lot of

28
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.190
development shifting towards prompting-based
development, developing

29
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.630
applications using LLMs, this
whole workflow evaluation process

30
00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:34.360
is being rethought. So, lots
of exciting concepts in this video.

31
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.650
Let's dive in.

32
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.553
Alright, so let's
get set up with evaluation. First, we

33
00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:43.154
need to have the chain
or the application that we're going

34
00:01:43.154 --> 00:01:45.118
to evaluate in the first place.

35
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.821
And we're going to use the
document question answering chain

36
00:01:48.821 --> 00:01:49.990
from the previous lesson.

37
00:01:50.591 --> 00:01:52.135
So we're
going to import everything we need.

38
00:01:52.796 --> 00:01:56.204
We're going to load the same
data that we were using.

39
00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:02.359
We're going to create that
index with one line.

40
00:02:03.141 --> 00:02:07.991
And then we're going to create the
retrieval QA chain by

41
00:02:07.991 --> 00:02:12.401
specifying the language model, the
chain type, the retriever, and

42
00:02:12.401 --> 00:02:16.370
then the verbosity that we're going
to print out.

43
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.258
So we've got this application, and
the first thing we need to do is

44
00:02:21.258 --> 00:02:23.738
we need to really
figure out what are

45
00:02:23.738 --> 00:02:26.837
some data points that
we want to evaluate it on.

46
00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:29.202
And so there's
a few different methods that we're going

47
00:02:29.202 --> 00:02:30.083
to cover for doing this.

48
00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:33.971
The first is the most simple,
which is basically we're

49
00:02:33.971 --> 00:02:36.977
going to come up with
data points that we think

50
00:02:36.977 --> 00:02:38.179
are good examples ourselves.

51
00:02:39.001 --> 00:02:43.564
And so to do that, we can just look
at some of the data and come up

52
00:02:43.564 --> 00:02:45.711
with example questions and
then example ground truth

53
00:02:45.711 --> 00:02:47.859
answers that we can
later use to evaluate.

54
00:02:49.742 --> 00:02:53.290
So if we look at
a few of the documents here,

55
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.806
we can kind of get a sense of what's
going on inside them. It

56
00:02:57.806 --> 00:03:01.676
looks like the first one, there's this
pullover set, there's this in

57
00:03:01.676 --> 00:03:06.514
the second one, there's this jacket, it
has a bunch of details about all of

58
00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:06.836
them.

59
00:03:08.399 --> 00:03:11.001
And from these details,
we can create some

60
00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:12.628
example query and answer pairs.

61
00:03:13.229 --> 00:03:16.145
So the first one,
we can ask a simple,

62
00:03:16.145 --> 00:03:19.061
"Does the Cozy Comfort
Pullover Set have side pockets?".

63
00:03:19.061 --> 00:03:22.948
And we can see by
looking above that it does, in fact,

64
00:03:22.948 --> 00:03:24.892
have some side pockets in it.

65
00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:28.579
And then for the second one,
we can see that this

66
00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:31.137
jacket is from a certain collection,
the DownTek collection.

67
00:03:31.137 --> 00:03:34.832
And so we can ask the question,
"What collection is this jacket from?"

68
00:03:34.832 --> 00:03:34.832


69
00:03:35.473 --> 00:03:38.795
And have the answer be,
"The DownTek collection". So here we've

70
00:03:38.795 --> 00:03:39.702
created two examples.

71
00:03:41.124 --> 00:03:43.016
But this doesn't
really scale that well. It

72
00:03:43.016 --> 00:03:45.381
takes a bit of time
to look through each example

73
00:03:45.381 --> 00:03:48.455
and figure out what's going
on. And so is there a way that

74
00:03:48.455 --> 00:03:49.401
we can automate it?

75
00:03:50.122 --> 00:03:52.413
And one of the
really cool ways that

76
00:03:52.413 --> 00:03:55.563
we think we can automate it is with
language models themselves.

77
00:03:55.563 --> 00:03:59.000
So we have a chain in
LangChain that can do exactly that.

78
00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:01.685
So we can import
the QA generation chain.

79
00:04:02.266 --> 00:04:04.709
And this will take
in documents and it

80
00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:07.457
will create a question
answer pair from each document.

81
00:04:08.218 --> 00:04:10.845
It'll do this using a
language model itself. So

82
00:04:10.845 --> 00:04:13.764
we need to create this
chain by passing in the

83
00:04:13.764 --> 00:04:14.932
Chat OpenAI language model.

84
00:04:15.653 --> 00:04:18.398
And then from there,
we can create a bunch of examples.

85
00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.520
And so we're going to use the
apply and parse method

86
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.890
because this is applying
an output parser to

87
00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:27.261
the result because we
want to get back

88
00:04:27.261 --> 00:04:30.224
a dictionary that has
the query and answer pair, not

89
00:04:30.224 --> 00:04:31.410
just a single string.

90
00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.043
And so now, if we look at
what exactly is returned here,

91
00:04:41.043 --> 00:04:44.731
we can see a query,
and we can see an answer, and let's

92
00:04:44.731 --> 00:04:48.135
check the document that this is
a question and answer for, and

93
00:04:48.135 --> 00:04:52.106
we can see that it's asking
what the weight of this is, we can

94
00:04:52.106 --> 00:04:55.226
see that it's taking the
weight from here, and look at

95
00:04:55.226 --> 00:04:58.346
that! We just generated
a bunch of question-answer pairs. We didn't

96
00:04:58.346 --> 00:05:01.466
have to write it
all ourselves. Saves us a bunch of

97
00:05:01.466 --> 00:05:05.154
time, and we can do more
exciting things. And so now, let's go

98
00:05:05.154 --> 00:05:08.557
ahead and add these examples into the examples
that we already created.

99
00:05:08.557 --> 00:05:08.557



100
00:05:09.599 --> 00:05:13.681
So, we got these examples now,
but how exactly do we evaluate what's

101
00:05:13.681 --> 00:05:14.309
going on?

102
00:05:15.351 --> 00:05:17.814
The first thing we want
to do is just run

103
00:05:17.814 --> 00:05:21.016
an example through the chain,
and take a look at the output it

104
00:05:21.016 --> 00:05:21.262
produces.

105
00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:26.020
So here we pass in a query,
and we get back an answer. But,

106
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.396
this is a little
bit limiting in terms

107
00:05:28.396 --> 00:05:31.662
of what we can see that's
actually happening inside the chain.

108
00:05:31.662 --> 00:05:31.662


109
00:05:32.283 --> 00:05:35.643
What is the actual prompt that's
going into the language model? What

110
00:05:35.643 --> 00:05:38.723
are the documents that it retrieves?
If this were a more

111
00:05:38.723 --> 00:05:40.963
complex chain with multiple
steps in it, what

112
00:05:40.963 --> 00:05:42.083
are the intermediate results?

113
00:05:42.864 --> 00:05:47.395
It's oftentimes not enough to
just look at the final answer to understand

114
00:05:47.395 --> 00:05:50.880
what is or could
be going wrong in the chain.

115
00:05:51.441 --> 00:05:57.145
And to help with that, we have a fun
little util in LangChain called

116
00:05:57.145 --> 00:05:57.553
"langchain.debug".

117
00:05:59.897 --> 00:06:05.200
And so if we set "langchain.debug =
True", and we now rerun

118
00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.410
the same example as above.

119
00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.363
We can see that
it starts printing out

120
00:06:10.363 --> 00:06:11.564
a lot more information.

121
00:06:12.466 --> 00:06:16.583
And so if we look at what exactly it's
printing out, we can see that it's diving

122
00:06:16.583 --> 00:06:18.278
down first into the
retrieval QA chain.

123
00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:22.075
And then it's going down into a
stuff documents chain. And

124
00:06:22.075 --> 00:06:24.370
so as mentioned, we're using
the stuff method.

125
00:06:25.011 --> 00:06:29.257
And now it's entering the LLM chain, where
we have a few different inputs. So

126
00:06:29.257 --> 00:06:31.805
we can see the original
question is right there.

127
00:06:32.466 --> 00:06:34.847
And now we're
passing in this context. And

128
00:06:34.847 --> 00:06:38.417
we can see that this context is created
from a bunch of

129
00:06:38.417 --> 00:06:40.202
the different documents that we've retrieved.


130
00:06:40.763 --> 00:06:44.673
And so when doing question answering,
oftentimes when a wrong result is

131
00:06:44.673 --> 00:06:47.931
returned, it's not necessarily the language
model itself that's messing

132
00:06:47.931 --> 00:06:50.863
up. It's actually the
retrieval step that's messing up.

133
00:06:50.863 --> 00:06:50.863


134
00:06:51.505 --> 00:06:53.768
And so taking a
really close look at

135
00:06:53.768 --> 00:06:56.881
what exactly the question is,
and what exactly the context is,

136
00:06:56.881 --> 00:06:58.579
can help debug what's going wrong.

137
00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:02.551
We can then step
down one more level

138
00:07:02.551 --> 00:07:05.644
and see exactly what
is entering the language model,

139
00:07:05.644 --> 00:07:06.675
Chat OpenAI itself.

140
00:07:07.437 --> 00:07:09.552
And so here we can
see the full prompt that's

141
00:07:09.552 --> 00:07:11.244
passed in. So we've got
a system message.

142
00:07:12.387 --> 00:07:15.149
We've got the description of
the prompt that's used. And so

143
00:07:15.149 --> 00:07:18.162
this is the prompt that the question answering
chain is using under

144
00:07:18.162 --> 00:07:20.170
the hood, which we
actually haven't even looked

145
00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:20.924
at until now.

146
00:07:21.425 --> 00:07:23.910
And so we can
see the prompt printing out,

147
00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.671
"Use the following pieces of
context to answer the user's

148
00:07:26.671 --> 00:07:29.156
question. If you don't know
the answer, just say

149
00:07:29.156 --> 00:07:33.298
that you don't know, don't try to make
up an answer." And then we see

150
00:07:33.298 --> 00:07:36.612
a bunch of the context as
inserted before, and then we see

151
00:07:36.612 --> 00:07:40.754
a human question, which is the question
that we asked it. We can also see

152
00:07:40.754 --> 00:07:42.963
a lot more information
about the actual return

153
00:07:42.963 --> 00:07:46.000
type. So rather than just
a string, we get back a

154
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.762
bunch of information like
the "token_usage", so the "prompt_tokens", the

155
00:07:48.762 --> 00:07:50.142
"completion_tokens", "total_tokens", and the "model_name".


156
00:07:50.142 --> 00:07:50.142


157
00:07:50.964 --> 00:07:54.475
And this can be really useful
to track the tokens that you're

158
00:07:54.475 --> 00:07:57.986
using in your chains or calls to language
models over time and

159
00:07:57.986 --> 00:08:00.619
keep track of the
total number of tokens, which

160
00:08:00.619 --> 00:08:02.668
corresponds very closely to
the total cost.

161
00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:06.061
And because this is
a relatively simple chain,

162
00:08:06.061 --> 00:08:10.012
we can now see that the final response,
"The Cozy Comfort Pullover Set,

163
00:08:10.012 --> 00:08:12.748
Stripe does have side pockets.",
is getting bubbled up

164
00:08:12.748 --> 00:08:16.091
through the chains and
getting returned to the user. So we've

165
00:08:16.091 --> 00:08:19.435
just walked through how to
look at and debug what's going

166
00:08:19.435 --> 00:08:21.866
on with a single input
to this chain.

167
00:08:22.447 --> 00:08:24.467
But what about all
the examples we created?

168
00:08:24.467 --> 00:08:26.235
How are we going to evaluate those?

169
00:08:26.996 --> 00:08:30.483
Similarly to when creating them,
one way to do it would be manually.

170
00:08:31.024 --> 00:08:35.083
We could run the chain over all the
examples, then look at the outputs,

171
00:08:35.083 --> 00:08:38.851
and try to figure out what's
going on, whether it's correct, incorrect, partially

172
00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:41.170
correct. Similar to
creating the examples, that starts

173
00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.490
to get a little bit
tedious over time.

174
00:08:44.171 --> 00:08:47.417
And so let's
go back to our favorite solution. Can

175
00:08:47.417 --> 00:08:51.961
we ask a language model to do it? First,
we need to create predictions

176
00:08:51.961 --> 00:08:54.882
for all the examples. Before
doing that, I'm actually

177
00:08:54.882 --> 00:08:58.777
going to turn off
the debug mode in order to just not

178
00:08:58.777 --> 00:09:00.724
print everything out onto
the screen.

179
00:09:01.466 --> 00:09:04.560
And then I'm going to create
predictions for all

180
00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.310
the different examples. And
so I think we

181
00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:11.435
had seven examples total, and
so we're going to loop through this

182
00:09:11.435 --> 00:09:14.530
chain seven times, getting a
prediction for each one.

183
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.830
Now that we've got these examples, we
can think about evaluating them.

184
00:09:35.331 --> 00:09:39.689
So we're going to import the QA, question
answering, eval chain. We

185
00:09:39.689 --> 00:09:43.685
are going to create this
chain with a language model, because

186
00:09:43.685 --> 00:09:47.680
again, we're going to be using a
language model to help

187
00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:48.770
do the evaluation.

188
00:09:51.825 --> 00:09:55.443
And then we're
going to call evaluate on this chain. We're

189
00:09:55.443 --> 00:09:59.060
going to pass in examples
and predictions, and we're going to

190
00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:03.006
get back a bunch of
graded outputs. And so, in order to

191
00:10:03.006 --> 00:10:07.281
see what exactly is going on
for each example, we're going to loop

192
00:10:07.281 --> 00:10:07.939
through them.

193
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.545
We're
going to print out the question, and

194
00:10:10.545 --> 00:10:12.509
again, this
was generated by a language model.

195
00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:15.284
We're
going to print out the real answer, and

196
00:10:15.284 --> 00:10:18.696
again, this was also generated by
a language model when it had the whole

197
00:10:18.696 --> 00:10:21.134
document in front of it, and
so it could generate

198
00:10:21.134 --> 00:10:22.109
a ground truth answer.

199
00:10:23.171 --> 00:10:25.411
We're going to print out
the predicted answer, and

200
00:10:25.411 --> 00:10:27.899
this is generated by a
language model when it's doing

201
00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:31.134
the QA chain, when it's doing the
retrieval with the embeddings and the

202
00:10:31.134 --> 00:10:33.872
vector databases, passing that into
a language model, and then trying

203
00:10:33.872 --> 00:10:35.116
to guess the predicted
answer.

204
00:10:36.238 --> 00:10:38.902
And then we're
also going to print out the grade, and

205
00:10:38.902 --> 00:10:40.597
again, this
is also generated by a

206
00:10:40.597 --> 00:10:43.744
language model when it's asking
the eval chain to grade what's going on

207
00:10:43.744 --> 00:10:45.197
and whether it's correct or incorrect.


208
00:10:45.999 --> 00:10:49.716
And so, when we loop through all
these examples and print them out,

209
00:10:49.716 --> 00:10:52.290
we can see those in detail for each
example.

210
00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.294
And looks like here
it got everything correct. This is a

211
00:10:58.294 --> 00:11:01.271
relatively simple retrieval problem,
so that is reassuring.

212
00:11:02.434 --> 00:11:06.339
So let's look at the first example. The
question here is, "Does

213
00:11:06.339 --> 00:11:09.269
the Cozy Comfort Pullover
Set have side pockets?". The

214
00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:11.873
real answer, and
we created this, is "Yes".

215
00:11:12.374 --> 00:11:15.895
The predicted answer,
which the language model produced was,

216
00:11:15.895 --> 00:11:19.809
"The Cozy Comfort Pullover Set,
Stripe does have side pockets".

217
00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:23.256
And so we can understand
that this is a correct answer.

218
00:11:23.757 --> 00:11:25.619
And actually the language
model does as well,

219
00:11:25.619 --> 00:11:26.783
and it grades it correct.

220
00:11:27.584 --> 00:11:30.413
But let's think about why we actually
need to use the language

221
00:11:30.413 --> 00:11:31.592
model in the first place.

222
00:11:32.193 --> 00:11:36.001
These two strings
are actually nothing alike.

223
00:11:36.682 --> 00:11:40.679
They're very different. One's really
short, one's really long. I don't even

224
00:11:40.679 --> 00:11:45.009
think, yes doesn't appear anywhere in this
string. So if we were to

225
00:11:45.009 --> 00:11:48.673
try to do some string
matching, or exact matching, or even

226
00:11:48.673 --> 00:11:51.337
some regexes here,
it wouldn't know what to

227
00:11:51.337 --> 00:11:55.667
do. They're not the same thing. And that
shows off the importance of

228
00:11:55.667 --> 00:11:59.664
using the language model to do
evaluation here. You've got these answers,

229
00:11:59.664 --> 00:12:02.329
which are arbitrary strings.
There's no single one

230
00:12:02.329 --> 00:12:05.326
truth string that is the
best possible answer. There's

231
00:12:05.326 --> 00:12:08.324
many different variants. And
as long as they have

232
00:12:08.324 --> 00:12:11.321
the same semantic meaning,
